\begin_layout Standard
The top 3 bits give the memory block number.
 Bits 0-12 give the offset of the line within the block.
 However,
 it would take 14 bits to span a 16k block,
 and there are only 13 left.
 So to convert bits 0-12 into the address of a screen line,
 you have to use a formula such as 
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
address = (offset & 7) + 2 * (offset & 0x1FF8) ;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Once you have the address of a screen line,
 it consists of 90 bytes,
 at intervals 8 apart (so a line would be stored in bytes 0,8,16,24,32...).
 This allows 8 lines to be interleaved for ease of printing text.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Interrupts
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Various peripherals can generate interrupts.
 When checking the source of an interrupt,
 PCW CP/M checks in the order FDC,
 timer,
 other peripherals.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Timer interrupts
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The timer interrupts 300 times a second.
 It also increments a counter which can be read from the bottom 4 bits of port F4h.
 The counter goes from 0-15,
 and stays at 15 having got there.
 Reading the counter resets it.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This allows PCW system software to compensate for missed timer interrupts.
 On interrupt,
 it reads the counter,
 and if it is more than 1 then it knows some interrupts have been missed.
 If it is 0,
 then the timer did not interrupt;
 the interrupt must be from a different source.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Floppy controller interrupts
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The floppy controller can be programmed to send a normal interrupt or a non-maskable interrupt (NMI) on completing a command.
 See Section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec: floppyf8"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If the floppy controller has tried to interrupt (regardless of whether it is set to produce an NMI,
 a normal interrupt,
 or nothing at all) then bit 5 of the value read from port F8h will be 1.
 Otherwise it will be 0.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Serial port interrupts
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The CPS8256 interface (see section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec: cps8256"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

) can be programmed to generate interrupts.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Daisywheel interrupts
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The daisywheel printer (see section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec: daisy"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

) can be programmed to interrupt when it has finished a command.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Printer ports
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
PCW8256/8512/9256/10 printer controller
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
I/O ports
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The PCW uses two I/O ports to communicate with the printer controller - ports 0FCh and 0FDh.
 0FCh appears to be used to initialise the controller,
 while 0FDh controls the printer itself.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
FCh
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset

(input) returns a controller error number.
 The PCW XBIOS only reads this when the controller reports an error (see bit 0 of port FDh).
 Values that can be returned are:
\begin_inset Separator latexpar
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Description
0 Underrun
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
1 Printer RAM fault
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
3 Bad command
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
5 Print error
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
0F8h Normal operation (no error).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If this port returns any other value,
 then 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

No printer
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 is displayed as an error.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Description
FCh
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset

(output) is used to send commands to the printer controller.
 Each command is a multiple of 2 bytes long.
 The meaning of the commands sent to ports FCh and FDh appears to be the same,
 but commands are only written to port FCh while the printer is being reset.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
FDh
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset

(input) returns the status of the printer.
 The meanings of the bits are as follows:
\end_layout

