\begin_layout Description
FDh
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset

(output) is used to send commands to the printer.
 All printer commands are sent to this port except during a reset.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Printer commands 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Printer commands are a multiple of 2 bytes long.
 Commands which are longer than two bytes end with the two bytes C0,00.
 Parameters use a vertical resolution of 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{1}{360}$
\end_inset

in;
 the horizontal resolution depends on the speed the motor is running at,
 but is either 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{1}{720}$
\end_inset

in or 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{1}{1440}$
\end_inset

in.
 In the following descriptions,
 the unit of horizontal measure is called the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

tick
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
00,00 First initialisation command sent to port 0FCh during boot up;
 possibly does a self-test.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
A1,nn Move the print head a small distance.
 Not followed by a C0,00 command.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Standard
It is possible that this command has A0,nn / A2,nn / A3,nn variants like the A8/A9/AA/AB commands below,
 but A1 is the only one I have observed in the wild.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The second byte of the command gives the distance to move,
 in ticks divided by 12.
 The CP/M printer driver only uses this command for moves of less than 156 ticks.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Description
A4,nn Line feed by 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{nn}{360}$
\end_inset

inches.
 Used only for feeds of 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{1}{30}$
\end_inset

in and less (ie,
 n <= 12).
 Not followed by a C0,00 command.
 It is possible that there are A5/A6/A7 variants for half-speed and reverse line feeds,
 but I haven't seen the PCW printer driver generate them.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
A8,nn;A9,nn;AA,nn;AB,nn Move the print head and print a line.
 Bits 0 and 1 of the command byte give the print head speed and direction:
\begin_inset Separator latexpar
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Description
bit
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset

0 If set,
 print head moves to the right;
 else left.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
bit
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset

1 If set,
 print head moves at half speed.
 If not,
 full speed.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The second byte of the command says by how many ticks the head should move in the direction of travel before the first output is printed.
 The CP/M printer driver appears to assume that between this command and the C0 00 that ends the command,
 the head will move 155 further ticks.
 For example,
 if it wants to move the head 216 ticks it will send a request to move by 61 ticks:
 A9 3D C0 00.
 In order to get bidirectional print to align in JOYCE,
 I had to split this value of 155 into 67 ticks at the start of the block and 88 ticks at the end.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This command is then followed by a number of data commands.
 These are of two kinds - to print,
 or to move the head by a specified number of ticks.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The command to print a column is treated as a 16-bit big-endian word with the following bitfields:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Bits 15-12 are 0.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Bits 11-9 give the column spacing.
 0 is 5 ticks,
 1 is 6 ticks,
 ...,
 7 is 12 ticks.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Bits 8-0 are 1 to fire the pins.
 Bit 8 is the bottom pin,
 bit 0 is the top.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The other type of command moves the head.
 These are normally at the end (to put the print head in the right place for the next row).
 The bit pattern for this type of command is:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Bits 15-13 are 1,0,0 (so this command is between 80h and 9Fh).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Bits 12-0 are the number.
 If the low 8 bits are 0,
 add 256.
 This means the commands,
 in increasing numerical order,
 are:
 80,01 80,02 ...
 80,FF,
 80,00,
 81,01 etc.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Finally,
 the command ends:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
C0,00 End of command.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Description
AC,nn Line feed by 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{n}{360}$
\end_inset

 inches.
 If nn is 0,
 feed 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{256}{360}$
\end_inset

inches.
 To feed more than that,
 pass further commands where bits 15-13 are 1,0,0 and bits 12-0 are the encoded number to feed - eg:
 AC,30 92,00 C0,00.
 It is possible that there are AD/AE/AF variants for half-speed and reverse line feeds,
 but I haven't seen the PCW printer driver generate them.
\begin_inset Separator latexpar
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Standard
This command is also terminated by a C0,00 end sequence.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Description
B8,00 Reset printer.
 Moves the print head to the left margin.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
C0,00 end of command sequence.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Example command sequences
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
00,00 A4,01 { A9,C0 } B8,00 - sent to port 0FCh as initialisation sequence.
 The A9,C0 pair is sent if the print head is against the left margin after the initial reset;
 this ensures that wherever the head was,
 its ability to move has been tested.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
C0,00 B8,00 - sent to port 0FCh as printer reset sequence.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
AC,3D C0,00 - line feed at 6 lines/inch
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
AB,86,0E,data,0E,data,...,0E,data,
 02,00,
 ...
 (21 times) ...,
 80,
 04,
 C0,00 - print a line of graphics in the ESC L graphics mode.
 The AB,86 starts printing and moves the head 134 + 67 = 201 ticks to the right.
 The commands starting 0E are the graphics columns,
 spaced 12 ticks apart.
 The 21 02,00 commands and the 80,04 move the head 130 ticks to the right of the last column,
 and the C0,00 moves it a further 88 ticks.
 Combined with the lead-in on the next line,
 this leaves the head ready to print under the first unprinted column of this line.
\end_layout

