\begin_layout Subsubsection
CPS8256 Centronics port
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The Centronics port (described in section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec: cps_cen"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

) returns its status in register 0 of Channel B.
 The STROBE signal is controlled by bit 7 of register 5 (what the DART thinks is DTR) and the BUSY signal shows up in bit 5 (CTS) of register 0.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
CPS8256 Serial port
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The serial port is connected to Channel A of the DART.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
To check if the port can output,
 read register 0 of Channel A.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
If you are using software handshaking,
 check bit 2 (Transmit buffer empty) and wait until it's nonzero.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
If you are using hardware handshaking,
 check bit 5 (Clear to Send);
 when this goes to 1,
 read register 1 until bit 0 (All Sent) is 1.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Then,
 to output,
 write the correct byte to the data channel.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To read from the port in non-interrupt mode:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
If hardware handshaking is enabled,
 check bit 0 of register 0 (RX character available) and if it's 0,
 raise DTR (set bit 7 of register 5).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Wait until bit 0 of register 0 becomes 1.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Read the character from the DART data channel.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
If hardware handshaking is in use,
 drop DTR.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To set transmit baud rate,
 send 36h to port E7h,
 and two bytes encoded rate to port E4h.
 To set the receive baud rate,
 send 76h to port E7h,
 and the two bytes to port E5h.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The encoded rate is 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{125000}{baud}$
\end_inset

 - send the high byte first,
 then the low byte.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
SCA Mark 2 Interface
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The SCA interface is programmed in the same way as the CPS8256,
 except that there's also a Real Time Clock fitted.
 The RTC is not emulated in JOYCE so this information has not been verified;
 use it at your own risk.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To read a byte from the RTC:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Set channel A DTR.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Reset channel B.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
For each bit:
 Unset channel A DTR,
 read the channel B Ring Indicator,
 and set channel A DTR.
 The bit obtained should be complemented.
 The first bit read is bit 7 of the byte;
 the last is bit 0.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To write a byte to the RTC:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Set channel A DTR.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
For each bit,
 if it's a 1 then reset channel B.
 If it's a zero set channel B RTS.
 Then reset and set channel A DTR.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Finally,
 unset channel A DTR,
 reset channel B,
 read a single bit from the channel B Ring Indicator,
 and set channel A DTR again.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To send an RTC command:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
To start the command:
 Unset channel A DTR,
 reset channel B,
 set channel B RTS then set channel A DTR.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Send the command bytes using the write code above.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Read any result bytes using the read code above.
 For all result bytes except the last:
 Reset channel B,
 set channel B RTS,
 unset channel A DTR,
 set channel A DTR and then reset channel B again.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Finally,
 set channel B RTS,
 set channel A DTR,
 unset channel A DTR and then reset channel B.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To read the clock:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Send the command D0h,
 0.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Send the command D1h;
 the next 4 bytes read will be hours,
 minutes,
 days,
 months (BCD).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Send the command D0h,
 5.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The clock will return 3 bytes.
 The first is the year (BCD);
 I suspect the others are day and month again,
 which we ignore.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To write to the clock:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Send the command D0h,
 20h.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Send the command D0h,
 hours,
 minutes,
 days,
 months (all BCD).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
or:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Send the command D0h,
 5.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Send the command D0h,
 year,
 day,
 month (BCD).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Early SCA interface
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
An older version of the SCA interface (presumably,
 the Mk1 as opposed to the Mk2) was not compatible at a hardware level with the CPS8256.
 Instead,
 SCA would supply a patch to CP/M;
 the resulting startup file would be named M14CPM3.EMS rather than J14CPM3.EMS.
 I don't have either the interface or the patch,
 so can't say how they differed.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec: llhardware"

\end_inset

LocoLink interface
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
LocoLink is used to connect the PCW to the parallel port of another computer.
 Usually this would be a PC,
 but it could also be a PcW16 and third-party software existed allowing a PCW9512 to do it (thus allowing transfers between 3
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 and 3.5
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 PCWs).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In early versions,
 the PC would act as a file server (
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

slave
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 in LocoLink terminology),
 and the PCW as the client (the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

master
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

).
 A utility run on the PCW would transmit files to the PC.
 Later versions reversed the roles,
 with the PCW acting as the file server and the other computer treating it as one or more disc drives.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Although the LocoLink interface connects to the parallel port,
 it acts as a serial device,
 using only 4 wires (two each way).
 This is so that it can support non-bidirectional parallel ports.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
On a PCW with the interface,
 the LocoLink appears at address 0FEh.
 The port behaves as follows:
\end_layout

