\begin_layout Standard
JOYCE v2.1.0+ can emulate the PCW end of a LocoLink connection.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The communications protocol used by the LocoLink interface is described in Appendix 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec: llproto"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
PCW Linkit
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The PCW Linkit interface is a similar device to the LocoLink (a 2-bit communication channel) except that both ends connect to PCW expansion ports.
 It appears at port 0FFh.
 The values are carried by the two low bits of the port;
 the value read by one PCW is the binary inverse of the value written by the other PCW.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The communications protocol used is outlined in Appendix 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:linkit"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
PCWMail
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The PCWMail connection is a cable designed to be used in conjunction with the PCW's MAIL232 software to transfer files between two PCWs.
 Consequently,
 from the point of view of the PCWs it behaves like two CPS8256 interfaces connected back-to-back,
 with a null-modem cable in between.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Miracle Technology WS4000
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
According to reviews,
 this was a modem that connected directly to the PCW expansion port.
 Other models of the same modem were sold either with conventional serial interfaces,
 or direct interfaces for different computers such as the Spectrum.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
My guess is that it emulated at least the serial port functionality of the CPS8256 and so would have appeared to the PCW as a normal serial port with a modem permanently connected to it.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Prototype serial interface
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The original PCW specification mentions that the prototype PCW had a serial interface based on the Intersil IM6403 UART.
 Its data register was at address 0FEh for both input and output,
 and its status register at 0F9h (input only).
 JOYCE does not emulate this interface.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Floppy drives
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The PCW floppy controller is the uPD765A,
 run in non-DMA mode.
 The FDC main status register is at I/O port 00h;
 its data register is at 01h.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec: floppyf8"

\end_inset

The floppy controller and other ports
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The System Control port (0F8h) has five commands which affect the floppy controller:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
02 If the floppy controller interrupts,
 the Z80 gets an NMI.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
03 If the floppy controller interrupts,
 the Z80 gets a normal interrupt.
 If the Z80 has disabled interrupts,
 the interrupt line stays high until the Z80 enables them again.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
04 Floppy controller interrupts are ignored.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
05 Set the floppy controller's 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Terminal count
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

,
 which aborts a data transfer.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
06 Clear the floppy controller's terminal count.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Floppy drive support
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
PCW operating systems support two floppy drives,
 connected to the controller as drives 0 and 1.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
On a PCW 8256,
 8512 or 9512,
 commands for drives 2 and 3 are sent to drives 0 and 1 (ie,
 the drive number is not completely decoded).
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
On a PCW 9256,
 9512+ or 10,
 commands for drive 2 appear to go to drive 1,
 except that the drive is always 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

ready
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

,
 even when the motor isn't running.
 As far as I can see from the schematic,
 selecting drive 3 selects both drives simultaneously.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Floppy controller probe
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Versions of LocoScript and CP/M written after the PCW9256 was released contain code to discover what sort of PCW is in use - 8256/8512/9512 or 9256/9512+/10.
 The code then detects whether 3.5
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 disc drives are in use.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The test is done in two stages.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Detect floppy controller type
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Stop disc motors.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Send SENSE DRIVE STATUS for drive 0 to the disc controller until it reports drive 0 is not ready.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Send SENSE DRIVE STATUS for drive 2.
 On an 8256,
 8512 or 9512,
 this returns the same status as drive 0 (ie:
 not ready).
 But on a 9256,
 9512+ or 10,
 it returns a status of 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

ready
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Detect drive type
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If a 9256-style controller was found,
 separate checks are then done on each drive to see whether they are 3.5
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 or not.
 The test is performed once on drive 0 and once on drive 2.
 The test on drive 2 will return results that are valid for drive 1,
 for some strange reason.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Start disc motors.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Recalibrate the drive (ie,
 move the head to track 0).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Send SENSE DRIVE STATUS to the drive.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
If the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Track 0
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 bit is not set:
\begin_inset Separator latexpar
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
For drive 0:
 If the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

read-only
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 bit is set,
 the drive is 3
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

.
 Otherwise it is 3.5
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
For drive 2:
 The drive does not exist.
 
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
If the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Track 0
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 bit is set:
\begin_inset Separator latexpar
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Turn off the disc motors,
 and wait for them to stop.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Send SENSE DRIVE STATUS to the drive again.
 If 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Track 0
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 is set,
 the drive is 3
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

.
 Otherwise it is 3.5
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Section
Hard drives
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
JOYCE does not emulate real PCW hard drives,
 so this information is not verified.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Cirtech Gem
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Physically,
 the Gem drive (at least the one I have) is a Seagate ST351A/X IDE drive,
 jumpered for XT mode.
 If the jumpers are set for AT mode it is possible to connect it to a modern PC and access the data at a sector level.
 Jumper settings for the ST351A/X can be found on Dell's website:
 <http://support.euro.dell.com/support/edocs/dta/18814/00000003.htm>.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In XTA mode the drive has four registers,
 mimicking an XT hard drive controller.
 On the PCW they can be found at ports 0A8h to 0ABh.
 The information below is adapted from the Interrupt List entry for ports 0320h-0323h,
 which is where the registers appear on a PC.
 For full programming information,
 see the IBM PC technical reference,
 pages 1-187 to 1-201.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
0A8h Data register.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
0A9h When read:
 controller status.
 When written:
 Reset controller.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
0AAh When read:
 controller DIP switches.
 When written:
 Generate controller-select pulse.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
0ABh When written:
 DMA and interrupt mask (not used on the PCW)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In addition,
 the Gem interface has a 4k boot ROM.
 It would appear that on initial startup,
 memory accesses with A7 reset go to the boot ROM,
 while memory accesses with A7 set go to the PCW mainboard.
 So the 4k ROM is mapped into memory from 0000-007F,
 0100-017F,
 0200-027F,
 ...,
 1F00-1F7F.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The first thing the GEM boot rom does is to mimic the normal PCW boot process by copying the standard boot image (section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:Boot-ROM"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

) into memory.
 It does this by repeated memory reads (from address 80h,
 so with A7 set) until the sequence D3 F8 [OUT (0F8h),A] is encountered.
 Then it executes its own write to that port to switch to normal execution,
 and copies itself into RAM.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
While the ROM is paged in,
 all I/O port accesses use 16-bit I/O [OUT (C),A style],
 with the top 8 bits of the address set to 80h.
 The first instruction after the boot ROM has been copied into RAM is IN A,(0A9h),
 with A=0.
 Presumably this I/O read,
 with the top 8 bits of the address all 0,
 pages the boot ROM out.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The format of the Gem drive boot track is documented in Appendix 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:The-Gem-Drive"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In a Gem-2 (mirrored) configuration,
 the 'main' drive is drive 1 (ie,
 jumpered as slave) and the 'backup' drive is drive 0 (ie,
 jumpered as master).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Cirtech Gem (ATA)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
I've also seen (but not really studied) a Gem drive based on a Conner Peripherals CP3024,
 which is ATA rather than XTA.
 The ATA registers would be mapped at 0A8h-0AFh:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
0A8h Data
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
0A9h Error / Features
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
0AAh Sector count
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
0ABh Sector number
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
0ACh Cylinder low
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
0ADh Cylinder high
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
0AEh Drive / Head
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
0AFh Status / Command
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
with the interface presumably converting between the 16-bit transfers expected by the drive,
 and the 8-bit transfers done by the Z80.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Cirtech Insyder / Hardpak
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
These are pretty much Gem drives in different form factors (the Insyder is fitted inside a PCW9512,
 and the Hardpak includes a 2.5
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 hard drive in the interface box).
 I have examined an Insyder,
 which again was an ATA drive.
 Before a transfer,
 the driver writes 0 to port 0FF94h,
 which may be to prepare whatever mechanism converts between 8-bit and 16-bit transfers.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
On the disk,
 sectors are stored with a non-obvious byte arrangement.
 Each 512-byte sector is stored as 256 little-endian words:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The high bytes of words 0-127 give bytes 0-127 of the sector.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The low bytes of words 0-127 give bytes 128-255 of the sector.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The high bytes of words 128-255 give bytes 256-383 of the sector.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The low bytes of words 128-255 give bytes 384-511 of the sector.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Cirtech Flash Drive
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The Flash Drive is an adaptation of the Gem drive concept,
 except that both hard drive and boot ROM are replaced with one or two Intel E28F008SA flash chips.
 There is also a physical switch to disable booting from the Flash Drive,
 allowing the PCW to be booted as normal if the drive does not contain a valid boot image.
 In normal use the drive is read-only;
 special utilities are supplied to erase it and copy files to it.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The drive is paged in by a read from I/O port 0xxD8h,
 with the high byte of the address specifying which 32k bank to select.
 This mechanism would allow a Flash Drive of up to 8Mb.
 However the supplied utilities are only aware of the 1Mb / 2Mb configurations.
 Reading from I/O port 0xxD9h pages the drive out again.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
When the drive is paged in,
 the selected memory bank is visible at 00h-7Fh,
 0100h-017Fh,
 0200h-027Fh ...
 0FF00h-0FF7Fh.
 Code that is accessing the drive,
 and any transfer buffers,
 must therefore be between addresses 0xx80h-0xxFFh.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Because of the physical design of the interface,
 the data lines for the first chip are connected in reverse order (the Z80's bit 0 is the chip's bit 7).
 This doesn't matter for data reads and writes,
 but the supplied utilities have to cope with this when issuing commands and reading the status register.
 For example,
 to check the 'low voltage' indicator in the status register the utilities have to look at bit 5 for the first chip,
 bit 3 for the second.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If the 'autoboot' switch is turned on,
 then when the PCW is booted bank 0 of the drive is mapped into memory,
 and therefore should contain a valid boot program.
 As with the Gem drive,
 its first action is to read the standard boot image into memory,
 by repeated memory reads from 80h.
 Similarly,
 port I/O is performed in 16-bit style with the bit 15 of the port address set until the standard boot image has been read,
 and 8-bit style subsequently.
 On the Gem drive this would page out the boot ROM,
 but as the Flash drive does not have a separate boot ROM I suspect this may just be legacy code.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The format of the Flash drive boot track is documented in Appendix 
