\begin_layout Description
Vortex
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset

System
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset

2000:
 A Miniscribe 8450 XT.
 I've seen this described as connected through a SCSI interface,
 but the 8450 XT drive uses XTA,
 not SCSI (though apparently some versions used the same 50-pin connector as SCSI drives).
 The same drive was sold for other systems,
 notably the Amiga,
 Atari ST and IBM PC,
 using an appropriate 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

personality module
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 to interface to the host computer.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
Cirtech
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset

Diamond:
 An earlier version of the Gem,
 but based on a SCSI interface rather than IDE.
 Among its features was the ability to connect up to seven PCWs to a single drive,
 taking advantage of the ability of the SCSI bus to support multiple controllers.
 I have no more technical details,
 but Cirtech's SCSI drives for Apple computers used an NCR 5380 so they may have used the same controller for the Diamond.
\end_layout

