\begin_layout Standard
There are three consecutive tables of font data:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
The character width table.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This is always 64 bytes long,
 and used for spacing the characters when proportional mode is selected.
 To get the width for character <n>,
 read byte (<n>/2);
 the high nibble will be for the even-numbered character (0,2,4,...) and the low nibble for the odd-numbered one (1,3,5,...) 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
The NLQ font
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The font starts with a table (the character offset table) containing 129 words.
 Each word has the following bitwise structure:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
Bit
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset

15:
 The character has a descender;
 print it on the bottom 8 pins rather than the top 8.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
Bits
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset

14-12:
 Space to put at the left of this character.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
Bits
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset

11-0:
 Offset of the specification of this character,
 from the start of the font.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The last entry gives the offset of the first byte after the font.
 This means that for any character,
 the size of its description can be found by taking offset(char+1) - offset(char).
 In the standard fonts,
 offset(128) also gives the offset of the start of the draft font from the start of the NLQ font.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
After the character offset table is a pattern table.
 Each entry in it is two bytes long;
 the high byte is printed on the first pass,
 and the low byte on the second (or vice versa?
 The high byte is drawn slightly above the low one,
 anyway).
 The least significant bit corresponds to the top pin.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The length of the pattern table isn't that important,
 but in the standard fonts it can be deduced simply by subtracting 258 from the offset of character 0.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
After that we have the character descriptions.
 As mentioned above,
 for each character,
 its description is from offset(char) to offset(char+1).
 An entry is a stream of bytes.
 If the top bit is set,
 it means:
 Leave a blank column before printing this column.
 The values of the low 7 bits are:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
00h-79h:
 Multiply by 2 to get an offset into the pattern table.
 Then take the two bytes at that offset for the first and second pass.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
7Ah:
 The two bytes after this code are printed on the first and second pass.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
7Bh-7Fh:
 Repeat the next pattern (byte - 79h) times,
 putting a blank column before the second and subsequent repetitions.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
The draft font
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
As with the NLQ font,
 the draft font starts with a character offset table,
 in which every word is formed:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
Bit
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset

15:
 The character has a descender;
 print it on the bottom 8 pins rather than the top 8.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
Bits
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset

14-12:
 Space to put at the left of this character.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
Bits
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset

11-0:
 Offset of the specification of this character,
 from the start of the font.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The last entry gives the offset of the first byte after the font.
 Fonts can use memory up to and including 6BFFh.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
After the character offset table is a pattern table.
 Each entry in it is a single byte,
 since draft mode only prints one pass.
 The least significant bit corresponds to the top pin.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The length of the pattern table isn't that important,
 but in the standard fonts it can be deduced simply by subtracting 258 from the offset of character 0.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
After that we have the character descriptions.
 As mentioned above,
 for each character,
 its description is from offset(char) to offset(char+1).
 An entry is a stream of bytes.
 If the top bit is set,
 it means:
 Leave a blank column before printing this column.
 The values of the low 7 bits are:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
00h-79h:
 This is an offset into the pattern table.
 The bit pattern for the next column is the byte at that offset.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
7Ah-7Fh:
 Repeat the next pattern (byte - 78h) times,
 putting a blank column before the second and subsequent repetitions.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
(note that unlike the NLQ print,
 there is no literal bitmap type).
\end_layout

