\begin_layout Subsection
Matrix fonts in LocoScript 1.00-1.20
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The fonts are stored in the file MATRIX.STD on the boot disc,
 in the same order as in CP/M.
 The data start at an offset of 011Ah from the start of the file.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
LocoScript 1 is able to print 224 characters - numbers 0-127 and 160-255.
 These are stored as a single block of bitmaps in MATRIX.STD,
 which subtracts 32 from character codes above 160 to create its internal character index.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The design of MATRIX.STD as a separate file seems to imply that other dot-matrix fonts could be loaded in LocoScript 1.
 However the only alternative LS1 fonts I've seen (in Digita International's Supertype) simply patch the existing MATRIX.STD file.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Note that later versions of LocoScript 1 (v1.30+) use the same font format,
 but different file formats - see below.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
PS widths table
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
There are 224 characters rather than 128,
 so the PS widths table is 112 bytes long.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
NLQ font
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Since there are 224 characters,
 the offsets table is 225 words long.
 The offsets to characters start at 0;
 ie,
 what must be added is the address of the character description table,
 not the address of the font itself.
 The offset table is at 18Ah in the file,
 and the character description table is at 442h.
 The character description bytes are almost the same as in CP/M,
 but the special values of the low 7 bits are slightly different:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
00h-7Ah:
 Multiply by 2 to get an offset into the pattern table.
 Then take the two bytes at that offset for the first and second pass.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
7Bh:
 The two bytes after this code are printed on the first and second pass.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
7Ch-7Fh:
 Repeat the next pattern (byte - 79h) times,
 putting a blank column before the second and subsequent repetitions.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Draft font
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The draft font is at offset 1181h in MATRIX.STD.
 Its offsets table is again 225 words long.
 As in CP/M,
 offsets are based on the font address (1181h).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Z80 code
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The first 11Ah bytes of MATRIX.STD are the Z80 code that generates font bitmaps.
 Again,
 this seems to suggest that alternative fonts were planned;
 the people designing the characters would have been able to use any code they liked to generate them.
 Very similar code is used under CP/M.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The entry point is at the beginning of MATRIX.STD,
 and takes the following parameters:
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
 A = character
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
BC = address of this routine (MATRIX.STD must be prepared to be 
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
     loaded anywhere in memory).
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
DE = address of a byte to which character width should be written.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
On return,
 the registers should be:
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
 A = character
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
BC = address of the code that generates the character bitmaps
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
PS width of character stored.
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
DE = 1 + entry DE.
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
HL corrupt.
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
All other registers and flags preserved.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The character bitmap generation code should then be called with:
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
 A = character
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
BC = address of this routine
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
DE = address of 24-byte buffer in which to store the generated character bitmap.
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
H = 0FFh for NLQ,
 else draft.
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
Bit 0 of L is 0 for pass 1,
 1 for pass 2 (or vice versa?)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
On return,
 the registers should be:
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
BC IX corrupt.
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
DE incremented by number of bytes written to the buffer.
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
All other registers and flags preserved.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Matrix fonts in LocoScript v1.31
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The MATRIX.STD file in this version of LocoScript has a 128-byte header,
 so 80h needs to be added to all offsets.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Matrix fonts in LocoScript v1.40-1.50
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
These versions of LocoScript doesn't have a MATRIX.STD file.
 Instead they have a PRINTER.JOY.
 The format of the data is the same,
 but 1D6Fh needs to be added to all offsets.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Matrix fonts in LocoScript 2-4
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In LocoScript 2,
 the dot-matrix printer font is stored in MATRIX.PRI (in versions prior to 2.20) and in MATRIX.#
\emph on
xx
\emph default
 font files (in versions 2.12 and later).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
There are three versions of these files:
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
1.1 LocoScript 2.00-2.11.
 Support for 480 characters.
 User-defined characters not supported.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
2.1 LocoScript 2.12-2.19.
 Support for 480 characters.
 User-defined characters supported.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
3.1 LocoScript 2.20+.
 Support for 512 characters.
 User-defined characters supported.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The format of the files is broadly similar;
 they start with a 128-byte header:
\end_layout

