\begin_layout Standard
Note that even in a v3 file (which defines 512 characters) there are only 480 entries in the PS widths table.
 Characters 256-287 are skipped.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The 4-byte index entry for each character is formed:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
DW
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset

offset The top 3 bits of the offset give the meaning of the remaining 13:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Description
Offset
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset

>=
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset

0E000h This is an accented character.
 Bits 13-0 of the offset give the base character number.
 Bits 4-0 of the flags byte give the accent character number.
 For example,
 character 200 (â) is stored as 61 E0 12 6B.
 Offset 0E061h means the base character is 61h ('a').
 Flags 12h mean the accent number is 2 (circumflex,
 character 3).
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
Offset
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset

0C000h-0DFFFh This is a duplicate character.
 Bits 13-0 of the offset give the duplicate character.
 For example,
 character 352 (capital Alpha) is stored as 41 C0 FF 57.
 Offset 0C041h means the shape from character 41h ('A') is used.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
Offset
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset

08000h-0BFFFh This is an uncompressed character (normally this would apply only to user-defined characters).
 Bits 13-0 of the offset give the address of its bitmaps relative to the start of the index table.
 The bitmaps are a standard size:
 11 bytes for draft,
 48 for NLQ.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
Offset
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset

00000h-03FFFh This is a standard character.
 Bits 13-0 of the offset give the address of its bitmaps relative to the start of the index table.
 The low 4 bits of byte 3 ('extra') give length of the 'draft' bitmap,
 and the remainder is the 'NLQ' bitmap.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Description
DB
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset

flags 
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Description
Bit
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset

7:
 Character shifted down.
 Shifted-down characters are printed on the bottom 8 printer pins,
 rather than the top 8;
 intended for characters with descenders.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
Bit
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset

6:
 Shift character down when superimposing an accent.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
Bit
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset

5:
 Character design cannot be copied in LocoChar.
 Set on the large mathematical symbols (large Pi,
 large Sigma etc.) because they are drawn in two halves,
 top and bottom.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
Bits
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset

4-0:
 For an accented character (offset >= 0E000h),
 the accent number.
 Otherwise:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
Bit
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset

4:
 When superimposing an accent,
 it replaces a dot (eg,
 on 'i' or 'j').
 Any non-blank line from the accent will completely replace the corresponding line from the character.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
Bits
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset

3-0:
 Proportional spacing columns minus 11
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Description
DB
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset

extra For a character where offset >= 0C000h,
 the low 8 bits of the end of the previous character's bitmap.
 Otherwise:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Description
Bits
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset

7-5:
 Blank columns at left (NLQ mode)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
Bit
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset

4:
 Blank columns at left (draft mode)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
Bits
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset

3-0:
 Number of bytes in draft character shape
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Standard
This code will compute the number of bytes in a character shape:
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
if (char[n].offset & 0x4000)  
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
{ 	
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
	return 0;	/* This character is a reference */ 	
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
} 	
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
else if (char[n+1].offset & 0x4000)  	
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
{ 	
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
	/* Next character is a reference */ 	
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
    unsigned offset2 = (char[n].offset & 0x1F00) | char[n+1].extra;
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
    if (offset2 < (char[n].offset & 0x1FFF)) offset2 += 0x100;
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
    length = offset2 - (char[n].offset & 0x1FFF);
 	
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
} 	
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
else 	
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
{ 	
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
	/* Next character is not a reference */  	
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
	length = (char[n+1].offset & 0x1FFF) - (char[n].offset & 0x1FFF);
 	
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
}
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For compressed characters,
 following the offset in the index table leads you to the draft-mode pattern.
 'extra' gives the length of the draft-mode pattern in bytes;
 add it to the offset to get the address of the NLQ pattern.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In the pattern,
 bit 7 of a byte is set if it should be preceded by a blank column.
 Bits 6-0 give a pattern number:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
00h-7Bh Bitmap reference.
 The bitmaps begin immediately after the index table;
 I have called this address index_end.
 
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Description
NLQ
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset

(v1):
 There is a table of bitmaps at index_end,
 containing 124 words.
 Within each word,
 the high byte is for the first pass and the low byte is for the second pass.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
NLQ
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset

(v2/v3):
 There are two sets of bitmaps,
 each containing 124 bytes.
 The bitmaps for the first pass are at index_end+7Ch and the bitmaps for the second pass are at index_end.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
Draft:
 There is a single set of bitmaps,
 at index_end+0F8h.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In all the tables,
 within each bitmap byte bit 0 is the top pin and bit 7 is the bottom pin.
 
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Description
7Ch Literal (only found in NLQ patterns).
 Followed by two bytes giving the pin bitmaps for the second and first passes.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
7Dh-7Fh Repeat.
 The following column is repeated value-79h times.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For uncompressed characters,
 following the offset in the index table leads you to 11 bytes giving the character bitmap for draft mode,
 followed by 48 defining it in NLQ mode.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The draft mode shape is 11 bytes.
 In each byte,
 bits 0-7 give the pin bitmap (bit 0 is the top pin,
 bit 7 the bottom).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The NLQ mode shape is 24 words.
 In each word,
 the high 8 bits give the pin bitmap on the first pass,
 and the low 8 bits the pin bitmap on the second pass.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec: llproto"

\end_inset

The LocoLink wire protocol
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
I have examined,
 or know of,
 the following versions of LocoLink:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
1.03
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset

/
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset

1.04:
 Distributed with LocoScript PC 1.x.
 A file server (LLPC.EXE) is run on the PC,
 and a command-line client program on the PCW (LLPCW.COM) is used to send files to the PC.
 The connection can either be through the LocoLink hardware,
 or a serial port.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
2.02:
 For use with LocoScript PC Easy and LocoScript Professional 2 Plus.
 The file server (LLINK202.EMS) is run on the PCW.
 The PCW then appears as a drive within the Disk Manager at the PC end.
 Serial support is dropped and the LocoLink hardware is required.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
3.0
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset

/
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset

LocoLink
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset

for
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset

Windows:
 This is mainly a file format conversion program,
 but it can also access the PCW through the LocoLink hardware.
 Although the hardware is the same,
 version 3 of the file server software is required at the PCW end.
 The main difference from version 2 is that version 2 shares a single drive and lets the user on the PCW choose which one is shared;
 version 3 shares all drives and the user on the PC chooses which to use within the LocoLink software.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
LocoLink16:
 When importing a file using the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Import
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 menu option,
 a PcW16 can access a non-16 PCW through the LocoLink hardware.
 This uses very similar client code to LocoLink for Windows (to the extent that I suspect the code was ported from one platform to the other).
 When in use,
 the PcW16's 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Import Document
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 screen gains checkboxes for 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

LocoLink (A/B)
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

,
 allowing documents from either drive of the PCW to be read in.
 Again,
 this requires version 3 of the file server at the PCW end.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
At the lowest level,
 the system for sending data through the LocoLink hardware seems to be the same in all known versions.
 The protocol appears to be symmetrical - ie,
 the server and the client go through the same steps to transmit a packet.
 However,
 it's easier to see what is going on at the PCW end,
 where the LocoLink interface presents the data directly to the CPU,
 than at the PC end where the parallel port interface gets slightly in the way - see section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "pcparport"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Basic concepts
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
LocoLink works with two wires in each direction - each computer can control the values of two,
 and read the values of the other two.
 The values taken together form a two-bit number (0-3) and it's most convenient to describe the protocol in these terms.
 For PCW - to - parallel communications,
 ACK is the high bit of the number and BUSY is the low bit.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "pcparport"

\end_inset

Bit mapping at the PC end
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
On a PC parallel port,
 these lines are swapped over (BUSY appears on bit 7 and ACK on bit 6).
 On the wire,
 the sense of both lines is inverted (it's 1 if the PCW is sending 0,
 and vice versa).
 Then when the PC reads the parallel port,
 the BUSY signal is inverted again:
 
\end_layout

