\begin_layout Standard
In theory longer versions of this packet might also contain SI,
 DI and BP.
 Most function calls don't need to pass parameters and so just send a 1-byte packet.
 For example,
 call 0Eh (log in drive) doesn't bother to send a drive number,
 since a LocoLink 2.x server computer provides only one disc drive.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
95h Requested data (see 7Eh below)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
0C3h Acknowledge data return (see 0ACh,
 0DAh below)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
It returns the following packet types:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
50h Result.
 Packet length is 0-8 bytes;
 its payload is the values for AX,
 BX,
 CX,
 DX (low byte first).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
67h Error.
 Packet length is 2 bytes;
 the first is the DOS error number (see INT 21h function 59h) and the second is 0.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
7Eh Request data.
 Used where the corresponding DOS function would pass data using a pointer or DMA.
 The packet contains 2 bytes.
 The first byte is a code giving the source of the data,
 the second is the maximum data size.
 The client will reply to this with a packet of type 95h containing the requested data.
 The source codes are:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Description
11h Fixed length - copied from DX in original function call
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
36h Fixed length - copied from DTA
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
5Bh Fixed length - copied from SI in original function call
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
80h ASCIIZ (eg:
 filename) - copied from DX in original function call
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
0A5h ASCIIZ (eg:
 filename) - copied from SI in original function call
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
0CAh ASCIIZ (eg:
 filename) - copied from DI in original function call
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
0EFh Fixed length - copied from immediately after previous parameter
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Description
0ACh Return data.
 Used where the corresponding DOS function expects data to be passed to it by a pointer or DMA.
 The packet type is 0ACh;
 the first byte is the location code (as for 7Eh,
 but only 11h,
 36h,
 5Bh and 0EFh are supported),
 and the remainder contains the data.
 The client will reply to this with a 0C3h packet,
 the first byte of which is 0ACh.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
0DAh Keep-Alive.
 Used when the server requires user intervention while servicing a call from the client (for example,
 it is displaying a 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Drive not ready
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 error message).
 Expects the client to acknowledge with a 0C3h packet,
 the first byte of which is 0DAh.
 This stops the client timing out waiting for a response from the server.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Once the server has sent a 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

success
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

error
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 packet (50h or 67h) the client can send another command packet or hangup packet.
\end_layout

