\begin_layout Description
Notes:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
*1 On LocoLink 3.00,
 attempts to get or change the drive label fail with a 'drive not ready' error.
 This is because the server requests the first 7 bytes of the extended FCB,
 but should request 8 (the eighth is the drive number).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
*2 These functions always return error 5,
 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

access denied
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

,
 since under CP/M user areas cannot be created or destroyed.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
*3 The current directory set by function 3Bh is returned by function 47h but it does not affect operations in any other way since (as mentioned above) absolute paths need to be passed to all the other functions.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
*4 LocoLink v3.x expects all pathnames to be absolute,
 eg A:
\backslash
USER.02
\backslash
PENNY.LS2.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
*5 The 'find next file' packet may either contain 1 byte (4Fh) or 17 bytes (4Fh,
 followed by bytes 0x06-0x15 from the find file data of the last file found).
 This is to allow depth-first search of directories;
 if LocoLink encounters a subdirectory,
 it will commence a new search to list files within that subdirectory.
 Once it has finished it will then send the long version of the find-next packet to resume the original search of the root directory where it left off.
 Bytes 0x06-0x14 of the find file data therefore need to contain enough information to identify which search is being continued.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
*6 Unlike 'find first file',
 the result of 'find next file' is returned in the result packet rather than as a separate transfer.
 It consists of bytes 0x11 onward of the find file data (but without the terminating zero byte on the filename;
 the caller has to deduce this using the packet length).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Emulated DOS filesystems
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
LocoLink 2.x and LocoLink for Windows / LocoLink 16 both present the PCW's CP/M filesystem as a DOS directory tree.
 How this is done depends on the selected display mode,
 LocoScript or CP/M.
 In LocoLink 2.x,
 this is set on the PCW.
 In LocoLink for Windows / PcW16,
 LocoScript or CP/M mode is selected on the PC and passed as a parameter to the 'reset disks' function.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
In LocoScript mode,
 the root directory of the filesystem is empty,
 except for subdirectories corresponding to the eight LocoScript groups.
 The subdirectory names will be taken from the group names (so EXAMPLE.GRP would become a subdirectory called EXAMPLE),
 or GROUP.
\emph on
n
\emph default
 if there is no .GRP file.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
In CP/M mode,
 user 0 becomes the root directory,
 and there will be up to 15 subdirectories named USER.01 to USER.15.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In LocoLink 2.x,
 there is also an option to hide empty user areas,
 which is selected by default.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
LocoLink 2.x,
 which supports only one drive,
 appears to use Z:
\backslash
 as the drive letter in absolute path names.
 LocoLink 16 uses A:
\backslash
 (and presumably B:
\backslash
 for the second drive,
 if one is present).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:linkit"

\end_inset

The PCW Linkit Wire Protocol
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The PCW Linkit interface,
 like LocoLink,
 has two lines in each direction.
 In this section I will call them H (high bit) and L (low bit).
 Unlike LocoLink,
 the protocol is symmetrical;
 either end can initiate a file transfer.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Establishing the link
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
As mentioned in the description of the hardware,
 the values read by one PCW are the inverse of those written by the other.
 When establishing the link,
 the Linkit software compensates for this by inverting the values immediately before they are written.
\end_layout

