\begin_layout Section
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:The-Timatic-Winchester"

\end_inset

The Timatic Winchester Expansion Box Partition Table
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The driver that I have seen Winchester Expansion Box assumes a 20Mb drive,
 with the partition table at logical sector 0xA28F.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
At offset 1Dh in the sector is a printable list of drives,
 six bytes per drive.
 Each entry reads 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

#:nnM,
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 with the last being 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

#:nnM.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 followed by CR / LF / LF / 0FFh.
 The driver will use this as a template for its startup message,
 replacing the '#' entries with the drive letters in use.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
At offset 64h in the sector is a table of CP/M DPBs,
 17 bytes per drive.
 These all report 4 128-byte sectors per track (that is,
 a single 512-byte sector per track) so the CP/M track number corresponds to the SASI sector number.
 This would only work on a drive size up to 32Mb.
 Following the last DPB is a 0FFh byte.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Byte 255 of the sector is set when written to the XOR of the previous 255 bytes,
 complemented.
 Thus when the first 256 bytes of the sector are XORed together,
 the result should be 0FFh.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Unused bytes in the sector are filled with the repeated text 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

C TURVEY.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 
\end_layout

